```java
class ManualWiring {
    static PaymentService createPaymentService() {
        PaymentGateway gateway = new StripeGateway(apiKey());
        return new PaymentService(gateway);
    }
}
```

This is a hand-written factory: it decides which concrete `PaymentGateway` to construct and wires it into `PaymentService`. Every class added to the graph needs its own wiring function like this one, written and maintained by hand.

```java
@Component
class PaymentService {
    private final PaymentGateway gateway;
    PaymentService(PaymentGateway gateway) { this.gateway = gateway; } // container supplies this
}

@Component
class StripeGateway implements PaymentGateway { /* ... */ }
```

The container scans for `@Component`-annotated classes, and when something needs a `PaymentService`, it resolves `PaymentGateway` first (finding `StripeGateway`), constructs it, then constructs `PaymentService` — the exact same factory logic as the manual version, just generated and run automatically for every class in the graph.

```java
class PaymentService {
    void charge(Order order) {
        PaymentGateway gateway = ServiceLocator.get(PaymentGateway.class); // hidden dependency
        gateway.charge(order);
    }
}
```

`PaymentService`'s constructor reveals nothing about needing a `PaymentGateway` — the dependency is fetched from a global registry deep inside a method body, which is exactly the hidden-dependency problem DI via constructor injection is meant to avoid.
